"""Labels for 1939 -> 1947 (the 1947 Code: complete re-write, Laws renumbered 1-47 and re-ordered, Notes merged into Laws).
Evidence: Rait Kerr 1950 (S16) ch.5 (purpose and terms of reference of the revision) and ch.6 (1947 entries u1-u5 and serials
129-138); ACS transcription of the 1947 Code (S09) as an independent text; project audits (S17 numbers, S18 text equivalence);
two contemporary press reprints (S39-01, S39-02) that could NOT be read directly (see new_sources_g9.csv), used only as low-weight
support. Many events here are the same text moved to a new Law number; a removed old Law is labelled as a merge and the
substantive change, if any, is labelled on the event holding the new Law."""
from _h import make
PAIR = "1939_1947"
L, LABELS = make(PAIR)
ST, UN, NC, SW = "STRUCTURAL", "UNCATEGORISED", "NOT_A_CHANGE", "SEMANTIC_WORDING"

D47 = ("1947 Code adopted at the MCC Special General Meeting of 7 May 1947, not to operate before the start of the 1948 English "
       "season (Rait Kerr ch.5 p.63); text confirmed in the ACS 1947 transcription (S09).")
TOR = ("Rait Kerr ch.5 (p.60-61): the terms of reference 'stressed the importance of a logical rearrangement, the incorporation in the "
       "Laws of appropriate matter hitherto given in the form of Notes, the provision of definitions wherever necessary, and the "
       "elimination of obscurities; changes of intent on major points were to be avoided'. This states the aim of the whole revision, "
       "not of this provision, so the basis is inferred.")
MERGE = "The removed Law's text reappears, re-worded, inside a renumbered 1947 Law (compare S09); "
RK_UMP = ("Rait Kerr ch.5 p.63: 'The only alterations in the conduct of the game occurred where actual experience had shown that the "
          "old Laws had caused difficulty for the umpires' (a statement about the revision as a whole).")
ELAST = ("Rait Kerr ch.5 p.60: 'the old code did not provide sufficient elasticity to meet the requirements of the various levels of "
         "competitive cricket, or of the differing climates in which the game had come to be played' (a reason for the revision as a whole).")
NOSRC = "No source gives a cause for this specific provision (searched Rait Kerr ch.5 and ch.6 serials u1-u5 and 129-138; ACS 1947)."

# ---------------- removed old Laws / sections ----------------
L("E001", "The 1939 'Notes for Scorers and Umpires' block is removed as a section; its content is redistributed: scorers must accept the umpires' signals and mutual consultation is permissible -> new Law 4 and its Note; 'in addition to signalling the umpire should call distinctly' -> Law 46 Note 3 ('an umpire must call as well as signal, if necessary'); the remaining paragraphs (score-sheet entries, signal code, captains to check the scores) survive in the 1947 'Notes for Scorers and Umpires'.",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", MERGE + "the rest of the old block is matched paragraph by paragraph in the 1947 appendix (separate relocation events). " + TOR,
  ["S16", "S09", "S18"], date=D47, note="Partly a segmentation artefact: the whole 1939 appendix is one removal record while its paragraphs are also matched as moves.")
L("E002", "The three 1939 'One-day Matches' Laws are removed and merged into the general Laws: 75-run follow-on -> Law 14; one-day match decided on first innings -> Law 22; umpires decide whether there is time to carry the game further -> Law 22 Note 2; tie -> Law 22 Note 4; declaration -> Law 15. The option 'Prior to the commencement of a match it may be agreed:- that the Over consist of 5 or 6 balls' is not carried over (1947 Law 23 allows 8 or 6 balls by agreed conditions).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.5 p.63 states the condensation itself: 'Sixty old Laws, including three for one-day matches, were condensed into forty-seven'. The loss of the 5-ball option for one-day matches has no stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E003", "The 1939 'Single Wicket' supplementary Laws (ten rules: one wicket and a bowling stump, bounds when fewer than five a side, three runs for lost ball, etc.) are dropped; no Single Wicket Laws appear in the 1947 Code (absent from the 1947 raw OCR and from the ACS 1947 transcription).",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "low", "none", NOSRC + " Rait Kerr's count of Laws condensed in 1947 mentions the one-day Laws but not the Single Wicket rules.",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E005", "Old Law 10 ('The Ball must be bowled; if thrown or jerked either Umpire shall call No Ball', and its Note on throwing at the striker's wicket) removed; merged into new Law 26 ('For a delivery to be fair the ball must be bowled, not thrown or jerked') and Law 26 Note 3.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "no change of substance in this part.", ["S09", "S16"], date=D47)
L("E008", "Old Law 11 (bowler's foot: 'with one foot on the ground behind the Bowling crease, and within the Return crease'; Notes requiring a foot 'grounded' and the heel-over-the-return-crease Note) removed; merged into new Law 26, where the grounding requirement is dropped (see the No ball Law label: Rait Kerr serial 133).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "the substantive change (back foot no longer required to be grounded) is labelled on the event holding the new No ball Law.",
  ["S09", "S16"], date=D47)
L("E013", "Old Law 14 (Changing ends: bowler may change ends as often as he pleases but not bowl two overs consecutively) removed; text moved into new Law 24.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "wording only.", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E015", "Old Law 15 (bowler may require the batsman to stand on the side of the wicket he directs) removed; text moved into new Law 24.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "wording only.", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E039", "Old Law 30 ('if he wilfully obstruct any fieldsman; Obstructing the field') removed; merged with old Law 26 into new Law 40 ('If he wilfully obstruct the opposite side').",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "the two obstruction Laws become one.", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E041", "Old Law 31 (Batsmen crossing: who is out when the wicket is put down) removed; the sentence is moved verbatim into new Law 41 (Run out).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "no change of substance.", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E043", "Old Law 32 (no run scored when the striker is caught; attempted run not scored on a run out; Note that crossed batsmen do not return) removed; merged into new Law 19 (Scoring) and its Note 1.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "no change of substance.", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E046", "Old Law 33A ('A Batsman being out from any cause, the ball shall be Dead') removed; merged into the new Dead Ball Law 25 ('or on a Batsman being out from any cause').",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, unnumbered, p.85): 'A new Law is included to collect and expand all the many references to the ball becoming \"dead\" previously scattered throughout the 1884 code.'",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E047", "Old Law 33B (ball lodging in a batsman's clothing is Dead) removed; merged into new Law 25 ('on lodging in the dress of either a Batsman or Umpire').",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, p.85): the new Law collects 'all the many references to the ball becoming \"dead\"'. The 1947 Law also extends lodging to the umpire's dress (previously a Note: lodging 'in his pocket or clothing').",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E052", "Old Law 38 (consent of the opposite side as to the person to act as substitute 'and the place in the field which he shall take') removed; merged into new Law 2, where the opposing Captain instead 'may indicate positions in which the Substitute shall not field' (Rait Kerr serial 129; labelled on the Law 2 event).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "the change of who names the fielding positions is labelled on the new Substitutes Law.", ["S09", "S16"], date=D47)
L("E057", "Old Law 40 ('A Batsman is liable to be out for any infringement of the Laws by his substitute') removed; replaced by Law 2 Note 2 ('An injured batsman may be Out should his runner infringe Laws 36, 40 or 41').",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "derived", MERGE + "the general liability becomes a list of the specific Laws (handled the ball, obstructing, run out).",
  ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E063", "Old Law 45 (Intervals: two minutes for each striker, ten minutes between innings, side refusing to play loses; Notes on in-going batsman, luncheon 40 minutes) removed; merged into new Law 17 (see that label for the changes, e.g. luncheon 45 minutes).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "the changes are labelled on the new Law 17 event.", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E069", "Old Law 47 (Jurisdiction of Umpires: bowler's-end umpire answers first except stumping, hit wicket, run out at the striker's wicket, Law 42; consultation Note) removed; merged into new Law 47 (Appeals) and its Notes.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "the exceptions are the same Laws under their new numbers (38, 42, 41).", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E079", "Old Law 48A (call No Ball instantly upon delivery, Wide as soon as it has passed the striker; Note on revoking a No Ball call) removed; merged into new Laws 26 and 28 and Law 26 Note 6.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "no change of substance.", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E080", "Old Law 49 (Short runs, with Notes on guard in front of the crease and two or more runs attempted) removed; merged into new Law 19 (Scoring) and its Notes 2-4, with a new 'One short' signal.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "no change of substance.", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E082", "Old Law 51 ('No Umpire shall be allowed to bet.') removed; the 1947 Code has no betting provision (new Law 3 instead requires umpires to control the game 'with absolute impartiality').",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "low", "none", NOSRC + " Whether 'absolute impartiality' was meant to cover betting is not stated anywhere; not asserted.",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E083", "Old Law 52 (no umpire changed without consent of both sides, 'except in case of violation of Law 51; then either side may dismiss him') removed; merged into new Law 3 ('No Umpire shall be changed during a match without the consent of both Captains'); the betting-dismissal clause goes with old Law 51.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "the dismissal clause is consequential on the removal of the betting Law.", ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E084", "Old Law 55 (no play on the first day(s) of a three- or two-day match: follow-on/declaration Laws apply as for a shorter match) removed; replaced by the general new Law 16.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "see the new Law 16 label.", ["S09", "S16"], date=D47)
L("E088", "Old Law 8 (Popping crease four feet from the wicket, unlimited in length; Note on measurement) removed; merged into new Law 9 (The Bowling and Popping Creases) and its Note.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", MERGE + "no change of substance.", ["S09"], date=D47)

# ---------------- new 1947 Laws ----------------
L("E004", "New Law 1 'Sides': 'A match is played between two sides of eleven players each, unless otherwise agreed' (from old Law 1) plus new rules: each side plays under a Captain who 'before the toss for innings shall nominate his players who may not thereafter be changed without the consent of the opposing [Captain]'; Note 1: a deputy must act if the captain is not available. (The 1939 Note on Law 37 only said the captains 'should exchange lists of the players' before tossing.)",
  ST, "codification_definition", "medium", "inferred", "A 1939 Note (exchange of lists to prevent abuse of substitutes) becomes a Law with a binding nomination rule. " + TOR,
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47,
  note="The dataset text ends 'consent of the opposing.' (word 'Captain' lost); ACS has 'opposing Captain'. The deputy-captain Note has no stated cause.")
L("E009", "New Law 11 'Covering the Pitch': 'The Pitch shall not be completely covered during a match unless special regulations so provide; covers used to protect the bowlers' run up shall not extend to a greater distance than 3½ feet in front of the Popping creases' (from 1939 Law 9 Note (c), 3 ft. 6 ins.); Note: covers removed 'early each morning, if fine' (1939: 'at 7 o'clock').",
  ST, "elasticity_for_conditions", "medium", "inferred", "A Note becomes a Law and the ban on complete covering is opened to 'special regulations'. Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, p.85): 'The term \"Special Regulation\" is defined in order to provide for any special playing conditions agreed upon as necessary to meet the requirements of differing climates, competitive cricket and so on.' Applied to this Law by inference.",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47, note="3½ ft confirmed by ACS 1947; same distance as 1939.")
L("E010", "New Law 12 'Maintenance of the Pitch': batsman may beat the pitch, players may use sawdust for footholds (from old Law 9), now 'provided Law 46 be not thereby contravened'; umpires to see foot-holes cleaned out and dried in wet weather (from old Law 9 Note (d)).",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", "Old Law text and Note regrouped as a Law of its own (logical rearrangement). " + TOR + " The new proviso points to the new Law 46 Note on players damaging the pitch (labelled there).",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E017", "New Law 16: 'When the start of play is delayed by weather Laws 14 and 15 shall apply in accordance with the number of days' play remaining from the actual start of the match' (replaces the case-by-case wording of old Law 55).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "inferred", "Rait Kerr serial 121 (1914/1919) already gives this principle: the follow-on and declaration rules 'are to apply in accordance with the number of days remaining after the actual start of play'; the 1947 Law restates it generally. " + TOR,
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E026", "New Law 22 'The Result' collects old Law 2 (winning, not compelled to continue), one-day Law 2, Law 54 Note (d) and the scorers' Note on captains checking the scores. New: a match not otherwise determined 'shall count as a Draw'; result stated as a win by runs or by wickets still to fall. The tie definition narrows: 1939 'scores are equal when time has been reached, but only if the match has been played out or if the side to bat last has commenced that innings' -> 1947 'A Draw is regarded as a Tie when the scores are equal at the conclusion of play but only if the match has been played out'.",
  ST, "codification_definition", "medium", "inferred", "A defining Law built from scattered Laws and Notes. " + TOR + " Dropping 'or if the side to bat last has commenced that innings' changes when a tie is recorded; no source explains it.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E028", "New Law 24: 'A Bowler shall finish an Over in progress unless he be incapacitated or be suspended for unfair play' (1939 Over Note (f) covered only injury), with the change-ends and side-of-wicket rules of old Laws 14 and 15.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "inferred", "Old Laws 14 and 15 and an Over Note grouped into one Law; 'suspended for unfair play' ties it to the short-pitched-bowling procedure in Law 46 Note 4(vi). " + TOR,
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E055", "New Law 4 'The Scorers': 'All runs scored shall be recorded by Scorers appointed for the purpose; the Scorers shall accept and acknowledge all instructions and signals given to them by the Umpires', with a Note on waiting for the signal to be answered and mutual consultation (from old Law 2 and the 1939 Notes for Scorers).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "inferred", "Matter from a Law and from the appendix Notes made into a Law of its own. " + TOR,
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E064", "New Law 45 (Duties of the Umpires): before the toss the umpires shall acquaint themselves with any special regulations, agree other conditions with both Captains, 'satisfy themselves that the wickets are properly pitched' (1939 Law 44: 'They shall pitch fair wickets') and agree on the watch or clock; Notes on other conditions of play and on captains knowing which clock is followed.",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", "Collects umpires' pre-match duties from old Laws 44 and 3 Notes. Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, p.85) records that 'The umpires are no longer required to pitch the wickets, but are to satisfy themselves that they have been properly pitched' without giving a cause. " + TOR,
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E086", "New Law 7 'The Pitch': 'The Pitch is deemed to be the area of ground between the bowling creases, 5 feet in width on either side of the line joining the centre of the wickets'; before the toss the ground executive selects and prepares it, thereafter the umpires control its use; it is not changed during a match unless unfit and with both Captains' consent.",
  ST, "codification_definition", "high", "inferred", "Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, p.85): 'A definition of the term \"Pitch\" is included, and the use of the term \"Wicket\" is confined to the stumps and the bails.' The 5-ft half-width was already in the 1939 drying regulations ('10 ft. in width, i.e., 5 ft. on each side from the centre stump'). " + TOR,
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)

# ---------------- revised Laws ----------------
L("E006", "Old Law 9 -> Law 10 (Rolling, Mowing and Watering): 'the ground shall be swept and rolled ... unless the In-Side object' becomes 'if the Captain of the batting side so elect, it may be swept and rolled' (7 minutes); rolling, mowing and covering now allowed 'by special regulations', which are defined (MCC for County cricket, Overseas Governing Bodies for their countries); 'Under no circumstances shall the Pitch be watered'; rolling time taken out of playing time after a late declaration (old Law 54 Notes: 'an extra seven minutes will be allowed'); mowing before the third day and on alternate days (1939: mown in week-end starts on the first day the match resumes).",
  ST, "elasticity_for_conditions", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, p.85): 'The term \"Special Regulation\" is defined in order to provide for any special playing conditions agreed upon as necessary to meet the requirements of differing climates, competitive cricket and so on.' The definition is in this event. The switch from rolling unless the batting side objects to rolling only if its captain elects, and the new mowing schedule, have no stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47, note="The 7 minutes dates from 1931 (Rait Kerr serial 126); unchanged.")
L("E007", "Rolling supervision Note rewritten (umpires responsible that rolling 'carried out at the request of the captain of the batting side' complies and ends in time), and the 1939 'Additional Law for Australia, New Zealand and South Africa' (groundman rolls up to ten minutes after any rain, whenever he thinks it will improve the wicket) becomes Law 10 Note 4: only 'if at any time a rain affected pitch is damaged by play thereon', on the umpires' instruction, under both umpires' supervision, at most one such rolling per day, and excluding normal rolling within two hours of the start.",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "low", "none", NOSRC + " The overseas after-rain rolling becomes narrower and umpire-controlled; no source explains why.",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E011", "Old Law 1 -> Law 13 (Innings): the number-of-players sentence moves to Law 1; toss now 'on the field of play'; new Notes: captains 'should toss for innings not later than 15 minutes before the time agreed upon for play to start'; 'The winner of the toss may not alter his decision to bat or field once it has been notified to the opposing captain'; the Law also governs a one-day match that continues after both first innings.",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "low", "none", NOSRC + " The toss timing and the irrevocable decision are new rules; the split of old Law 1 is part of the logical rearrangement (S16 ch.5).",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E012", "Cross-reference '(See Notes to Law 22.)' becomes '(See also Law 22.)': the pointer now refers to the new Result Law 22 instead of the old Caught Law 22.",
  ST, "consequential", "high", "derived", "A cross-reference updated for the renumbering; no rule changes.", ["S18", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E014", "Old Law 53 -> Law 14 (Following Innings): the one-day 75-run follow-on (from the One-day Laws) is added to the Law; '(in Australia 200 runs)' moves from the Law into a Note; revision dates removed. Margins (150/100/75) unchanged.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.5 p.63: 'Sixty old Laws, including three for one-day matches, were condensed into forty-seven'. The margins are those of serials 114 and 123.",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E016", "Old Law 54 -> Law 15 (Declarations): 'The In-side may declare their innings at an end' becomes 'The Captain of the Batting side may declare an innings closed'; three-day matches 'on the second and succeeding days' (1939: 'at any time on the second day'); revision dates removed; Notes dropped or moved: declaration in the luncheon interval or before play and the extra rolling time (-> Law 10 Note 3), 'A declaration of a first innings does not preclude a second innings', and the result of a declared innings (-> Law 22 Note 3).",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", "Law redrafted with its Notes redistributed. 'Succeeding days' matches Rait Kerr serial 124 (1927: the second-day rule extended to matches of more than three days). " + TOR + " Rait Kerr ch.5 p.63 also says the revision 'enabled several of the old Notes to be dropped'.",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E018", "Notes to old Law 16 (Scoring of No Balls and Wides) removed: a batsman cannot be stumped off a No Ball and the umpire decides between stumped and run out; runs off a No Ball scored to the striker; bowler allowed another delivery; byes or overthrows from a Wide scored as Wides. Their substance is now in Laws 27, 29, 41 and 42.",
  ST, "notes_revision", "medium", "inferred", "Rait Kerr ch.5 p.63: the inclusion of interpretations in the Laws 'enabled several of the old Notes to be dropped'. The stumped/run out off a No Ball distinction is the subject of serial 136 (labelled on the Run out and Stumped events).",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E019", "Old Laws 18 and 45 -> Law 17 (Commencement and Close of Play and Intervals): intervals for meals, 10 minutes between innings, 'not more than 2 minutes for each fresh batsman'; 'Play' called at the start of each innings, each day and after any interval; no trial ball 'to any player' (1939: 'to any bowler on the ground between the wickets'). New Notes: the umpires shall not award a match unless Play was clearly called, an appeal made and a side will not or cannot continue; the in-going batsman must pass the out-going one 'before the latter leaves the field of play' (1939: meet him before he enters the pavilion); luncheon 'should not exceed 45 minutes unless otherwise agreed' (1939: 'shall be 40 minutes'); 'Bowling practice on the pitch is forbidden at any time during the game.'",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "inferred", "Two old Laws and their Notes merged. " + TOR + " The longer luncheon, the practice ban and the conditions for awarding a match are real changes with no stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47, note="40 and 45 minutes checked in raw OCR (1939a/b raw; 1947 raw l.331) and ACS.")
L("E020", "Note on the last wicket falling just before luncheon (no allowance for the 10 minutes between innings) now applies to 'luncheon or tea' (1939: 'luncheon' only); 'two minutes' -> '2 minutes'.",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "low", "none", NOSRC + " The rule is extended to the tea interval.", ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E021", "Old Law 50 -> Law 18: umpires call 'Time' and remove the bails at every interval, the end of each day and the end of the match. 'An Over shall always be started if Time has not been reached, and shall be completed unless a batsman is Out or Retires within 2 minutes of the completion of any period of play, but the Over in progress at the close of play on the final day of a match shall be completed at the request of either Captain even if a wicket fall after Time has been reached' (1939 Over Note (e): if a wicket fell within two minutes of time, play continued only if 'the incoming batsman claims his right').",
  "ENJOYMENT", "fairness_spirit", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.5 p.62: 'the previous custom had been to leave a decision on this to the batting side in the case when a wicket fell within two minutes of \"Time.\" It was decided that this was a one-sided option, and it would be fairer to provide for the final over to be played out at the request of either captain' (consultation with Overseas Bodies and the Counties; serial 131). S39-02 (unverified press reprint of Wisden) is reported to give the same reason.",
  ["S16", "S09", "S39-02"], date=D47 + " Proposed in the draft circulated in 1945-46 and reported in the press in January 1947 (S39-01, unverified).")
L("E022", "Old Law 2 -> Law 19 (Scoring): the short-run rule (old Law 49) and the caught/run-out runs rule (old Law 32) are merged into the Law, with a 'One short' signal; Notes: crossed batsmen do not return except on a boundary or under Laws 30 Note 1 and 46 Note 4(vii); guard in front of the crease; one run deducted however many short; the scorers sentence moves to Law 4, the result and tie sentences to Law 22.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "inferred", "Three old Laws and their Notes merged into one. " + TOR, ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E023", "Old Law 37 -> Law 2 (Substitutes): 'no Substitute shall be allowed to bat or to bowl' moves from a Note into the Law; consent to the substitute obtained from the opposing Captain, 'who may indicate positions in which the Substitute shall not field' (1939 Law 38: consent 'as to ... the place in the field which he shall take'); Notes on the injured batsman with a runner moved from old Law 39; the courtesy and exchange-of-lists Notes dropped; 'A side should not field with more than eleven players' added.",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "low", "inferred", "Rait Kerr serial 129 records the change ('The batting captain has the right to say where a fielding substitute may not field; previously he was required to say where he should field. N.B. Subject to consent a substitute may keep wicket.') but gives no cause for it. " + RK_UMP,
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E024", "Old Law 44 -> Law 20 (Boundaries): 'Before the toss for innings the Umpires shall agree with both sides on the Boundaries' (1939: 'arrange boundaries where necessary'); umpire calls or signals 'Boundary' when the ball 'hits, crosses or is carried over' it; 'It is a Boundary if the ball touches any boundary line'; customary allowance 4 runs, usually 6 for hits pitching over and clear; runs completed count only if they exceed the allowance; on an overthrow or wilful act of a fieldsman runs made and the allowance are added; sight screens within the playing area count as boundary; the catching-at-the-boundary Note moves to Law 35.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "inferred", "Notes (a)-(i) of old Law 44 are absorbed into the Law and four Notes. " + TOR + " The requirement to agree boundaries with both sides is a small new procedure with no stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E025", "Old Law 34 -> Law 21 (Lost Ball): 'when the ball shall be Dead' removed from the Law (now in Law 25, Dead on 'any penalty being awarded under Laws 21 or 44'); 'six' -> '6'; Note 'A lost ball should be replaced by one in a similar condition' moved to Law 5 Note 3.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, p.85): 'A new Law is included to collect and expand all the many references to the ball becoming \"dead\" previously scattered throughout the 1884 code.'",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E027", "Old Law 13 -> Law 23 (The Over): 'Overs of six balls' (with 'For Australia and New Zealand only ... substitute eight') becomes 'overs of either 8 or 6 balls according to the agreed conditions of play', Note: 'In default of any agreement to the contrary, in the United Kingdom the over shall be 6 balls'; 'Over' called when 'both sides have ceased to regard the ball as in play' (from 1939 Note (a)); old Notes (a)-(g) removed or moved (ball not counting when bails blow off or ball slips -> Law 25 Note 2; finishing the over near time -> Law 18; injured bowler -> Law 24; change of mode of delivery -> Law 26 Note 2).",
  ST, "elasticity_for_conditions", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, serial 131, p.86): 'Latitude is provided for the \"Over\" to be of either 6 or 8 balls in order to meet the requirements of cricket overseas.'",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47, note="'8 or 6' confirmed by ACS 1947.")
L("E029", "Changing-ends rule moved into Law 24: 'as often as he pleases, provided only that he does not bowl' -> 'as often as desired, provided only that he shall not bowl'.",
  ST, "wording_only", "high", "derived", "Same rule re-worded on relocation.", ["S09", "S18"], date=D47)
L("E030", "Side-of-wicket rule moved into Law 24: 'to stand on that side of it which he may direct' -> 'to stand on whichever side of it he may direct'.",
  ST, "wording_only", "high", "derived", "Same rule re-worded on relocation.", ["S09", "S18"], date=D47)
L("E031", "Notes to the 1939 L.B.W. Law removed: out L.B.W. when bowling round the wicket; immaterial whether the ball breaks; not out if the ball touched bat or hand first (now in the Law); hat blowing off and deflecting the ball; 'person' includes the striker's dress (now Law 31 Note 5 for all Laws).",
  ST, "notes_revision", "medium", "inferred", "Rait Kerr ch.5 p.63: the inclusion of interpretations in the Laws 'enabled several of the old Notes to be dropped'. Two of these Notes survive inside other provisions; the others are dropped as self-evident.",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E032", "Old Law 35 -> Law 25 (Dead Ball): one list of when the ball is Dead (settled with keeper or bowler, boundary, lodging in batsman's or umpire's dress, Over or Time, batsman out, penalty under Laws 21 or 44); umpire calls 'Dead Ball' to intervene for unfair play, serious injury, or to suspend play before the striker receives a delivery; 'The ball shall cease to be Dead on the Bowler starting his run or bowling action'; Note 2 lists when play may be suspended (striker not ready for an adequate reason; ball dropped or not released; 'one or both bails' fall before delivery - 1939: 'both bails have been blown off'); bowler run-out Notes moved to Law 27 Note 2 and Law 46 Note 4(vii).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entries, p.85-86): 'A new Law is included to collect and expand all the many references to the ball becoming \"dead\"' and serial 132: 'The new \"Dead ball\" Law for the first time states when the ball ceases to be \"dead.\"' The new suspension cases (striker not ready; one bail falling) have no stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E033", "List label '(ii)' -> '(iii)' of a relocated sub-item.", NC, "segmentation_artefact", "high", "derived",
  "Only a list number differs; layout, not rule text.", ["S18"])
L("E034", "Old Law 48 -> Law 26 (No Ball): 'For a delivery to be fair the ball must be bowled, not thrown or jerked' (old Laws 10 and 48 merged), umpire to 'call and signal No Ball instantly upon delivery'; bowler's-end umpire calls No Ball unless 'at the instant of delivery the Bowler has at least some part of one foot behind the Bowling crease and within the Return crease, and not touching or grounded over either crease' (1939: 'one foot on the ground'; 'One foot must be grounded'). Notes: both feet behind the crease allowed; striker 'entitled to know' whether the bowler bowls over or round, overarm or underarm, right or left handed, and failure to notify may be called No Ball; throwing at the striker's wicket is a No Ball; breaking the near wicket in delivery is not; signal; revoke the call if the ball is not released.",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr serial 133: 'The \"No ball\" Law no longer requires the bowler to have his back foot \"grounded\" at the instant of delivery, though it must still be behind the line of the Bowling crease. Previously umpires had found it impossible to adjudicate on this point, and as a matter of course had disregarded the old instruction.' Notification of right/left hand and the No Ball sanction extend 1939 Over Note (g); no cause stated for that extension.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E035", "New Law 27 (No Ball, more) - paired by number with old Law 27 (Hit the ball twice), a Law-identity artefact of the renumbering. The new text: the ball does not become Dead on a No Ball call; scoring of runs from a No Ball (from old Law 16); striker out off a No Ball only under Law 37, either batsman run out or out under Laws 36 or 40; Notes: penalty scored only if no runs result; non-striker leaving too soon may be run out by any recognised method; bowler throwing at the near wicket is not a No Ball, runs so scored, the throw does not count in the Over (from 1939 Law 35 Note (c)).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "derived", "Old Law 16 and Notes to old Law 35 regrouped as the second No Ball Law; the old Hit the ball twice text it is paired with reappears in new Law 37 (separate event). The out-off-a-No-Ball list is the same set of dismissals as 1939 under new numbers.",
  ["S09", "S18"], date=D47)
L("E036", "Old Law 12 -> Law 28 (Wide Ball): Wide only if the ball 'passes out of reach of the Striker, and would not have been within his reach when taking guard in the normal position' (1939: 'it is not within reach of the Striker'); call and signal 'as soon as it shall have passed the Striker'; a ball coming to rest in front of the striker is not a Wide; signal; an umpire 'should revoke the call' if the striker hits a Wide (1939: 'the call shall be ignored').",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "medium", "inferred", "Rait Kerr serial 134: 'It is made clear that the striker cannot create a \"Wide\" by stepping back.' He gives no further cause; " + RK_UMP,
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E037", "New Law 29 (Wide Ball, more), built from old Law 16 and 1939 Wide Note (d): the ball is not Dead on a Wide call; all runs run are scored Wide Balls, else one run; striker may be out off a Wide under Laws 38 or 42, either batsman run out or out under Laws 36 or 40.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", "Same rules as 1939 (Note (d) listed Laws 23, 25, 28, 29, 30 = stumped, hit wicket, run out, handled, obstructing) under new numbers.",
  ["S09"], date=D47)
L("E038", "Old Law 3 -> Law 3 (Appointment of Umpires): appointed 'Before the toss for innings' (1939: 'Before the commencement of the match') 'one for each end to control the game as required by the Laws with absolute impartiality'; 'No Umpire shall be changed during a match without the consent of both Captains' (from old Law 52); Note: report to the executive of the ground 30 minutes before play. 1939 Notes (a)-(f) dropped or moved (where to stand -> Law 46 Note 1; watch or clock -> Law 45; no interference except on appeal -> Law 46 Note 4(i); checking wickets, bats and balls -> Law 46; scorer not to dictate -> Law 4).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "inferred", "Law and Notes regrouped. " + TOR, ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E040", "Old Law 17 -> Law 30 (Bye and Leg Bye): umpire 'shall call or signal'; a leg bye if the ball touches the striker's 'dress or person except his hand' (1939: 'person (hand excepted)'); Note (a) 'If the striker kicks the ball wilfully, no runs shall be scored' becomes Note 1 'If the striker wilfully deflects the ball with any part of his person, no runs are scored, and the batsmen may not change ends'; signals Note (from the 1939 signal code).",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "inferred", "Law, Note and signal regrouped. " + TOR + " Widening 'kicks' to any wilful deflection and adding that the batsmen may not change ends is a real change with no stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E042", "Old Law 20 -> Law 31 (The Wicket is Down): down 'if either the ball or the Striker's bat or person completely removes either bail from the top of the stumps' (1939: 'when either of the bails is struck off', with a Note that any disturbance of a bail sufficed); Note 1: 'A wicket is not down merely on account of the disturbance of a bail, but it is down if a bail in falling from the wicket lodges between two of the stumps'; any player may put the wicket down with hand or arm or pull up a stump if holding the ball (from 1939 Note (c)); 'person' includes dress and equipment.",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr ch.5 p.62: consultation 'pointed out the unsatisfactory nature of the custom that a wicket could be regarded as \"down\" if either bail was in the slightest degree disturbed, a custom which was interpreted in different ways by different umpires'; 'the Australian view that the criterion for the wicket to be \"down\" should depend on the actual fall of a bail was generally approved' (serial 135). S39-01 (unverified press reprint) reportedly describes the same draft change.",
  ["S16", "S09", "S39-01"], date=D47, note="OCR fragment '?\\ny' for 'Any' in the new text.")
L("E044", "Old Law 19 -> Law 32 (Out of his Ground): 'unless his bat in hand, or some part of his person be grounded within the line of the Popping crease' -> 'unless some part of his bat in hand or of his person be grounded behind the line of the Popping Crease'; the Notes (part of foot within the line suffices; foot on the line is out) dropped.",
  SW, "clarifying_rewrite", "medium", "inferred", "'Behind the line' says in the Law what the dropped Note said ('If the foot is on the line, but no part is within, the batsman would be out of his ground'); the rule is the same but the Law alone is now unambiguous about a foot on the line. Fits the aim of 'the elimination of obscurities' (S16 ch.5).",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E045", "Old Law 36 -> Law 33 (Batsman Retiring): 'A Batsman shall not retire from his wicket and return to it ... after another has been in, without the consent of the opposite side' -> 'A Batsman may retire at any time, but may not resume his innings without the consent of the opposing Captain, and then only on the fall of a wicket'; Note: retired through illness, injury or unavoidable cause recorded 'Retired, Not out', otherwise 'as a completed innings to be recorded as Retired, Out' (1939: such an innings 'must be counted as Not out').",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "low", "none", NOSRC + " Resumption only on the fall of a wicket and the 'Retired, Out' entry are new.", ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E048", "Old Law 21 -> Law 34 (Bowled): Law re-worded to the new 'The Striker is out Bowled' form; Note: out Bowled if after playing the ball 'he then kicks or hits it on to his wicket before the completion of his stroke' (1939: 'kicks the ball on to his wicket'); 1939 Note (a) (out Bowled when a stump is bowled down though the bails remain) dropped.",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", "Re-drafting into the 1947 form. " + TOR + " Adding 'or hits' and the completion-of-stroke limit, and dropping the stump-down Note, are small changes with no stated cause (the dropped Note sits uneasily with the new requirement that a bail be completely removed).",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E049", "Old Law 22 -> Law 35 (Caught): caught from 'a stroke of the bat or of the hand holding the bat' (1939: 'bat or hand'), 'held by a Fieldsman', or 'accidentally lodged in his dress'; 'The Fieldsman must have both his feet entirely within the playing area at the instant the catch is completed' (1939 Law 44 Note: a fieldsman within the ground 'even though he be touching the boundary' catching a ball outside it); Notes re-written: hand may touch the ground; catch completed before the ball touches the ground or is carried over the boundary stands; ball touching the striker's person does not invalidate; ball lodging in the keeper's pads; second-stroke catch only if the ball has not touched the ground; caught off any obstruction not decided as a boundary (1939: 'a tree, hedge or building' if no boundaries). 1939 Note (g) dropped.",
  ST, "codification_definition", "medium", "inferred", "Interpretations moved into the Law and Notes rewritten. " + TOR + " 'Hand holding the bat' matches the new Law 36 Note 1; the both-feet requirement is new; neither has a stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E050", "Old Law 29 -> Law 36 (Handled the Ball): 'if he touch with his hands or take up the ball while in play' -> 'If he touch it while in play with his hands'; new Note 1: 'A hand holding the bat is regarded as part of it for the purposes of Laws 36, 37 and 39.'",
  ST, "codification_definition", "medium", "inferred", "Adds a definition used by three Laws (provision of definitions, S16 ch.5). " + TOR, ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E051", "New Law 37 (Hit the Ball Twice) - paired by number with old Law 28 (Run out), a Law-identity artefact. The new text: out if he wilfully strikes the ball again 'except for the sole purpose of guarding his wicket'; 'No runs except those which result from an overthrow shall be scored from a ball lawfully struck twice'; umpire decides legitimacy, attempted run is evidence; Note 2: 'This Law is infringed if the striker, after playing the ball and without any request from the opposite side, uses his bat to return the ball to a fieldsman' (1939: 'The spirit of this Law is infringed' if he knocks it back to the bowler 'although his wicket is in no danger'); score-book entry Note.",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "derived", "Old Law 27 and Notes regrouped (its old Run-out pairing reappears in new Law 41). Turning a 'spirit of this Law' Note into an infringement (so the striker is out) is a real hardening with no stated cause.",
  ["S09", "S16"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E053", "Old Law 25 -> Law 38 (Hit Wicket): 'with his bat or any part of his person or dress' -> 'with his bat or any part of his person' ('person' now includes dress by Law 31 Note 5); Notes merged into one list (second stroke; cap or hat falling 'or by part of his bat'; not out while running).",
  ST, "reorganisation", "high", "inferred", "Same rules re-drafted; 'dress' covered by the general definition. " + TOR, ["S16", "S09"], date=D47,
  note="Dataset text has 'be hit down' (OCR for 'he hit down').")
L("E054", "Old Law 24 -> Law 39 (L.B.W.): the striker's person must be 'in a straight line between wicket and wicket, even though the point of impact be above the level of the bails'; the ball must not have 'first touched his bat or hand' (from a 1939 Note); the ball 'shall have, or would have, pitched' on the straight line (covering a full toss) or pitched on the off side; Note: four questions, now including '(and this case includes a ball intercepted full pitch by the striker)' and 'irrespective of the height of the point of impact'.",
  ST, "codification_definition", "high", "inferred", "Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, p.85): 'The \"L.B.W.\" Law is more closely defined in respect of a ball hitting the striker either full-pitch or above the level of the bails.' He gives no cause beyond the definition. " + TOR,
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47, note="The 1937 off-side extension (serial 128) is carried over unchanged.")
L("E058", "Old Laws 26 and 30 -> Law 40 (Obstructing the Field): 'Either Batsman is out ... If he wilfully obstruct the opposite side; should such wilful obstruction by either Batsman prevent a ball from being caught it is the Striker who is out'; Notes: umpire decides 'wilful'; 'The involuntary interception by a batsman while running of a throw in is not in itself an offence'; score-book entry 'when a batsman' (1939: 'when the striker').",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "inferred", "Two obstruction Laws merged (the 1884 extension, Rait Kerr serial 107, already covered either batsman). " + TOR + " The throw-in Note is a new interpretation without stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E059", "Old Law 39 -> Law 41 (Run Out) (old runner Law paired with the new Law): 'Either Batsman is out Run out - If in running or at any time, while the ball is in play, he be out of his ground, and his wicket be put down by the opposite side'; crossing rule (from old Law 31); 'But unless he attempt to run, the Striker shall not be given Run out in the circumstances stated in Law 42, even should No Ball have been called'; Note: ball played on to the opposite wicket needs a fieldsman's touch. The 1939 runner Notes move to Law 2 Note 2.",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr serial 136: 'The Laws regarding \"Running out\" and \"Stumping\" revert to the original eighteenth-century conception, in order to eliminate many difficulties of interpretation which previously existed. It is now ruled that when the wicket-keeper puts down the wicket, the striker is not to be given \"Run out\" unless in the opinion of the umpire he is attempting a run.' Ch.5 p.60: 'it was an enquiry from the Bradford Central Cricket League in 1943 on the case of a Run out off a \"No ball,\" arising from an obscure amendment to the Law in question, which was directly responsible for the decision to revise the code.'",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E060", "Old Law 23 -> Law 42 (Stumped): 'If in receiving a ball, not being a No Ball, delivered by the Bowler, he be out of his ground otherwise than in attempting a run, and the wicket be put down by the Wicket-keeper without the intervention of another fieldsman' (1939: 'in playing at the ball, provided it be not touched by the bat or hand'); 'Only when the ball has touched the bat or person of the Striker may the Wicket-keeper take it in front of the wicket for this purpose'; Note: may be stumped off a rebound from the keeper's person; one-bail and foot Notes dropped.",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr serial 136 (stumping and run out revert to the eighteenth-century conception 'in order to eliminate many difficulties of interpretation which previously existed') and serial 137 ('The old Laws were obscure on the point of the wicket-keeper taking the ball in front of the wicket').",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E061", "Old Law 42 -> Law 43 (The Wicket-keeper): 'shall stand behind the wicket' -> 'shall remain wholly behind the wicket until a ball delivered by the Bowler touches the bat or person of the Striker, or passes the wicket, or until the Striker attempts a run'; otherwise the striker is not out except under Laws 36, 37, 40 and 41 'and then only subject to Law 46'; the keeper's incommoding by noise or motion moves to Law 46 Note 4(iv) (any fieldsman); Note: the Law secures the striker's right to play the ball; he is not penalised for interfering with the keeper in legitimate defence.",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr serial 137: 'The old Laws were obscure on the point of the wicket-keeper taking the ball in front of the wicket. The Law now states that he must remain behind the wicket until the striker plays the ball, or the ball passes the wicket, or the striker attempts a run.'",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E062", "Old Law 41 -> Law 44 (The Fieldsman): 'the ball shall be Dead' removed (now in Law 25); 'five shall be added' -> 'five shall be scored'; new: 'The penalty shall be added to the score of the Striker if the ball has been struck, but otherwise to the score of Byes, Leg Byes, No Balls or Wides as the case may be'; new Note 'A fieldsman must not use his cap, etc., for the purpose of fielding a ball'.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "inferred", "Dead-ball wording moved to Law 25 (Rait Kerr 1947 entry on collecting dead-ball references). " + TOR + " Where the penalty runs are credited, and the cap Note, are new written rules with no stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E065", "Old Law 43 -> Law 46 (Duties of the Umpires): umpires 'shall ensure that the conduct of the game and the implements used are strictly in accordance with the Laws'; 'final judges' of ground, weather and light 'in the event of the decision being left to them'; change ends after each innings (from old Law 44). Notes: where to stand (not interfering with run up or view; inform the batsman); not influenced by players or spectators; call as well as signal; 'The use of resin, wax, etc., by bowlers is also unfair, but a bowler may dry the ball when wet on a towel or with sawdust'; 'An umpire is justified in intervening under this Law should any player of the fielding side incommode the striker by any noise or motion while he is receiving a ball'; 'The umpires are justified in preventing players from causing damage to the pitch which may assist the bowlers'; 'Any attempt by the batsmen to steal a run during the bowler's run up is unfair'. Dropped: reporting cases to the MCC Secretary; Notes on tricks and on throwing.",
  ST, "drafting_omission", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr serial 138: 'Previously no mention was made to the incommoding of the striker by any fieldsman except the wicket-keeper. This is now corrected by bringing obstruction of this nature within the judgment of the umpires in respect of unfair play.' The wax/towel, pitch-damage and run-stealing Notes are new with no stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E066", "Law 46 Note 4 (Fair and unfair play) wording: umpires may intervene without appeal 'but should not otherwise interfere with the progress of the game, except as required to do so by the Laws' (from old Law 3 Note (d)); 'a player criticising or showing resentment' -> 'a player failing to comply with the instructions of an umpire or criticising his decisions'; seam-lifting: 'the bowler' -> 'a player'; the umpire 'will if necessary change the ball for one which has had similar wear' (1939: the ball 'should be shown to the umpire who should call for another ball').",
  SW, "qualifier_scope", "medium", "inferred", "Re-wording that widens who is covered ('a player' for 'the bowler'; failing to comply with instructions) and specifies the replacement ball; it could change enforcement (non-bowlers lifting the seam, disobeying an umpire) without a stated change of policy. " + TOR,
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E067", "Short-pitched bowling procedure: 'he shall adopt' -> 'he must adopt'; 'he shall forthwith caution' -> 'he forthwith cautions'; 'he shall inform' -> 'he informs'; 'the umpire ... shall:-' -> 'must:-'; 'the over shall be regarded as completed' -> 'when the over is regarded as completed'; list labels 1-3 -> (a)-(c).",
  SW, "modal_strength", "medium", "inferred", "Modal words change ('shall' -> 'must' for the umpire's duty to act; the steps become indicative). The procedure (caution, inform captain, call Dead Ball, bowler taken off) is unchanged; 'must' may read as a firmer duty on the umpire. The procedure itself dates from the 1934-35 instructions on 'direct attack' (Rait Kerr ch.4 p.54-55).",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E068", "'A bowler who has been taken off as above shall not bowl again' -> 'may not bowl again'; ground, weather and light: 'Unless agreement to the contrary is made before the start of a match, the captains (during actual play the batsmen at the wickets may deputise for their captain) may elect to decide ... otherwise or in the event of disagreement, the Umpires are required to decide' (1939: batsman and fielding captain consult on weather and light, umpires decide if they disagree); new 'Play should only be suspended when the conditions are so bad that it is unreasonable or dangerous for it to continue'; unfit ground now includes the batsmen's foothold; inspection 'After any suspension of play' (1939: 'interruption from rain'); 1939 Notes on spectators' impatience and appeals at the start of a match dropped; new run-stealing procedure (umpire calls Dead Ball as soon as the batsmen cross, unless the bowler throws at a wicket; they return to their original wickets).",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "low", "none", NOSRC + " The 'unreasonable or dangerous' test, the extension to any suspension and the run-stealing procedure are new; the 'shall not' -> 'may not' change is a modal re-wording.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=SW, secsub="modal_strength", date=D47)
L("E070", "Old Law 46 -> Law 47 (Appeals): appeal must be made 'prior to the delivery of the next ball, and before Time is called under Law 18' (from old Law 50); bowler's-end umpire answers first except Laws 38, 42 and run out at the striker's wicket (from old Law 47); Notes: signals; 'An umpire may alter his decision provided that such alteration is made promptly' (1939: 'if, in the hurry of the moment or by a slip of the tongue he has given a wrong one'); new 'Nothing in this Law prevents an umpire before giving a decision from consulting the other umpire on a point of fact' (1939: appeal to the other umpire only when prevented from seeing); 'The umpire should intervene if satisfied that a batsman, not having been given out, has left his wicket under a misapprehension' (1939: 'not required to intervene save on appeal'); doubt goes to the batsman under Law 46; Over/Time Note; 'Law 4 explains the status of the scorers'.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "inferred", "Old Laws 46, 47 and 50 and their Notes merged. " + TOR + " Wider grounds to alter a decision, consultation on a point of fact and the duty to intervene are real changes with no stated cause.",
  ["S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47)
L("E071", "'the other umpire may answer any appeal within his jurisdiction, subject to an observance of Law 50' -> '..., provided it is made in time'.",
  ST, "consequential", "high", "derived", "Old Law 50 (time limit for appeals) was merged into the new Appeals Law, so the cross-reference is replaced by its meaning.", ["S09", "S18"], date=D47)
L("E072", "Notes for Scorers (score-sheet entries for No Balls and Wides) moved: '(Law 16)' -> '(Laws 27 and 29)'; 'Whatever runs' -> 'All runs'; list labels and OCR ('IT' for 'If').",
  ST, "consequential", "high", "derived", "Cross-reference renumbered; otherwise the same recommendations.", ["S18"], date=D47)
L("E073", "List labels '(i)' -> '(li)' and '(ii)' -> '(ii)' (OCR of '(ii)').", NC, "format_artefact", "high", "derived",
  "Only list labels differ, one of them an OCR misreading.", ["S18"])
L("E074", "Signal code in the Notes for Scorers re-set as a list: 'Boundaries shall be signalled by waving the hand from side to side' -> 'Boundaries-by waving the hand from side to side' (same for boundary six, byes, leg byes, no balls).",
  ST, "wording_only", "high", "derived", "Same signals, list layout instead of sentences.", ["S18"], date=D47)
L("E075", "Signals for Wides, 'Out' and 'One Short' in the signal code appear as additions; the same three signals are in the 1939 code ('Wides shall be signalled by extending both arms horizontally', etc.), only re-set as '-by' items.",
  NC, "segmentation_artefact", "high", "derived", "The 1939 text has the same three signals in the same appendix; the diff did not match the re-formatted lines.", ["S18"])
L("E076", "Scorers' Note 3 and the drying regulations moved: 'as stated in Law 2' -> 'Law 22'; title 'DRYING THE WICKETS' -> 'DRYING THE PITCH'; 'the artificial drying of the pitch, as defined above' -> 'of the pitch' and '(as defined in (b) (i) above)' removed (the pitch is now defined in Law 7); 'Law 43, Note (b)' -> 'Law 46, Note (5)'; OCR ('tit', 'eases').",
  ST, "consequential", "high", "inferred", "Cross-references and terms updated to the new numbering and the new Pitch definition (Rait Kerr ch.6, 1947 entry: 'A definition of the term \"Pitch\" is included'); the regulations are otherwise unchanged.",
  ["S16", "S18"], date=D47)
L("E077", "Drying regulations N.B. and paragraph 1 appear as additions; the same sentences are in 1939 ('These regulations are primarily designed for First-class Cricket ...'; 'Except as provided below, the existing regulations ... shall apply'), with the cross-reference '(See Laws 9 and 43.)' -> '(See Laws 10, 12 and 46)'.",
  ST, "consequential", "high", "derived", "Unmatched only because of re-segmentation; the one real difference is the renumbered cross-reference.", ["S18"], date=D47)
L("E078", "List label '(t/)' (OCR of '(d)') -> '(v)'.", NC, "ocr_noise", "high", "derived", "Only a list label, garbled by OCR in 1939.", ["S18"])
L("E081", "Old Law 4 -> Law 5 (The Ball): new ball 'Subject to agreement to the contrary either Captain may demand a new ball at the start of each innings' (1939: 'either side may demand'); umpires 'shall allow another ball to be taken into use' when one is lost or unfit and inform the batsmen 'whenever a ball is to be changed'; balls in First-class matches approved 'by the umpires and captains' (1939: captains, County cricket); 200-run new ball for First-class matches only (Minor Counties dropped), 'the captain of the fielding side may demand', other grades only 'if so agreed before the toss' (1939: 'optional'); replacement ball of similar wear. The dataset's circumference '9 inches, nor more than 9⅛ inches' is an AI clean-up invention: the raw OCR lacks the lower figure and ACS 1947 gives 8 13/16 to 9 inches, unchanged from 1939.",
  ST, "elasticity_for_conditions", "medium", "inferred", "Rait Kerr serial 130: 'The provision of a new ball at the start of an innings is no longer obligatory in every class of cricket.' He gives no cause for this entry; " + ELAST + " The size 'change' is not a change (S17, S09).",
  ["S16", "S09", "S17"], sec=NC, secsub="cleaning_artefact_numeric", date=D47, integrity="suspect",
  note="Ball size: see DATA_INTEGRITY_FINDINGS.md (1947 ball 9 to 9 1/8 in. invented). Weight 5½-5¾ oz is unchanged (words -> figures).")
L("E085", "Old Law 5 -> Law 6 (The Bat): 'four inches and one-quarter' -> '4¼ inches', 'thirty-eight inches' -> '38 inches'; '(See also Law 3, Note (e).)' dropped.",
  NC, "format_artefact", "high", "derived", "Words written as figures and a cross-reference to a dropped Note removed; same limits (ACS 1947: 4 1/4 in., 38 in.).", ["S18", "S09"])
L("E087", "'The wickets shall not be changed during a match, unless the ground between them become unfit for play, and then only by consent of both sides' -> 'The Pitch shall not be changed ... unless it becomes unfit for play, and then only with the consent of both Captains'.",
  ST, "terminology", "high", "inferred", "Rait Kerr ch.6 (1947 entry, p.85): 'A definition of the term \"Pitch\" is included, and the use of the term \"Wicket\" is confined to the stumps and the bails.' Same rule in the new terms.",
  ["S16", "S09"], date=D47)
L("E089", "Old Law 6 -> Law 8 (The Wickets): dimensions fixed for all cricket: width '9 inches' (1939: 'not less than eight inches nor more than nine', 9 in. in First-class), stumps 'with their top 28 inches above the ground' (1939: 27-28, 28 in First-class), bails 'each 4 3/8 inches' (1939: 4 to 4½, 4½ in First-class), i.e. First-class bails shortened by 1/8 inch; new Note 'Except for the bail grooves the tops of the stumps shall be dome-shaped'; metal fittings no longer 'permissible' wording ('should not be encouraged'); First-class Note removed; bail Note now points to Law 31.",
  ST, "consequential", "low", "inferred", "S39-01 (contemporary press reprint of The Times on the MCC draft; text not read directly, content known only from search summaries) reportedly says the bails were shortened by an eighth of an inch and the stump tops made dome-shaped to prevent bails jamming - which would make it consequential on the new rule that a bail must fall for the wicket to be down (Rait Kerr serial 135). Making the First-class dimensions compulsory for all cricket has no stated cause.",
  ["S39-01", "S16", "S09"], sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=D47,
  note="All figures confirmed by raw OCR (1947 raw l.180-190) and ACS 1947 (9 in., 28 in., 4 3/8 in., ½ in.). Upgrade the cause to 'stated' only if S39-01 is read and confirms it.")
L("E090", "Old Law 7 -> Law 9 (The Bowling and Popping Creases): popping crease 'marked 4 feet in front of and parallel with the Bowling crease' (from old Law 8); 'Both the Return and Popping creases shall be deemed unlimited in length'; Note on measuring from the centre of the stumps; Note 'The Return crease is shortened for convenience' dropped; figures for words.",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "high", "derived", "Old Laws 7 and 8 merged; dimensions unchanged (8 ft 8 in., 4 ft; ACS 1947).", ["S09"], date=D47)
